      "text": "To find the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\), we'll follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the prime factorization of \\(400\\):**\n   \\[\n   400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\):**\n   The formula for the sum of the divisors of a number \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n   \\]\n   Applying this to \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate each term in the product:**\n   \\[\n   1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 + 5 + 25 = 31\n   \\]\n\n4. **Multiply these results together:**\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n   \\]\n\n5. **Factorize \\(961\\) to find its prime factors:**\n   \\[\n   961 = 31^2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Identify the distinct prime factors:**\n   The prime factorization \\(961 = 31^2\\) indicates that the prime factor of \\(961\\) is \\(31\\) once.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) has \\(\\boxed{1}\\) distinct prime factor.",
