      "text": "First, let's find the prime factorization of 400.\nWe have $400 = 2^4 \\cdot 5^2$.\nThe sum of the positive divisors of a number can be found using the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Sum of divisors} = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}) \\]\n\nwhere $p_i$ are the prime factors of the number and $e_i$ are their respective exponents.\n\nFor 400, with prime factors $2$ and $5$, we have:\n\n\\[ \\text{Sum of divisors} = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) \\]\n\\[ = (1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25) \\]\n\\[ = 31 \\cdot 31 \\]\n\\[ = 961 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the prime factorization of 961.\nWe have $961 = 31^2$, which is a perfect square.\n\nSince 961 has only one distinct prime factor, 31, the sum of the positive divisors of 400 has $\\boxed{1}$ distinct prime factor.",
